WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.410 --> 00:00:09.690
Okay, I'm really glad to see Generation Data Groups or GDGs included in the list of topics

2
00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:14.410
for this course because I think they're just something that's a great example of a feature

3
00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:20.490
in ZOS that just makes perfect sense to have and it's executed in such a brilliant way

4
00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:23.290
you'll just wish it were available everywhere.

5
00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:27.030
So picture a program that gathers data during the day.

6
00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:31.570
Maybe it tracks problem tickets that were opened or guests checking into a hotel.

7
00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:35.170
No matter what, you've got a whole bunch of data being generated and every once in

8
00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:40.310
a while it's nice to start with a fresh data set so you're not opening up this huge thing

9
00:00:40.310 --> 00:00:41.850
each time.

10
00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:46.990
So you create a new data set and update the program to start looking in that and when

11
00:00:46.990 --> 00:00:50.890
you want to look for an old data set, you go through all the old ones and you find the

12
00:00:50.890 --> 00:00:52.030
one you want.

13
00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:53.570
It seems pretty simple.

14
00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:58.390
In fact, it's a pattern that you see so often on ZOS that we have a special way of dealing

15
00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:01.010
with that exact type of situation.

16
00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:06.510
A generation data group is a collection of historically related data sets that are arranged

17
00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:08.430
in chronological order.

18
00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:12.990
So you have that program writing out data and maybe your procedure is to cut over a

19
00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:15.410
new data set every month.

20
00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:19.610
Let's say we're in September so we've got August and July sitting in there as the minus

21
00:01:19.610 --> 00:01:23.030
one and minus two months relative to September.

22
00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:27.250
October comes along and here's where GDGs leap into action.

23
00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:31.630
We specify that you want a plus one generation of the data set.

24
00:01:31.630 --> 00:01:37.390
That moves September into the minus one slot and August down to the minus two slot.

25
00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:42.550
Now my program can continue referencing the same data set, generation zero, of the current

26
00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:43.830
version.

27
00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:48.190
And if we're going to do something like compare this month's numbers to last month's numbers,

28
00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:52.510
we just specify that we want to read from the minus one generation.

29
00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:55.430
All of the logic is handled by the GDG.

30
00:01:55.450 --> 00:02:00.170
We also get to specify just how many generations we want to keep around so it doesn't just

31
00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:03.410
keep growing until it runs out of space.

32
00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:09.010
Here you can see an example of specifying a GDG where we can add a new generation, use

33
00:02:09.010 --> 00:02:12.770
the current generation, or use an older generation.

34
00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:16.250
The example down here is creating a new generation.

35
00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:19.290
That's called using a relative data set name.

36
00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:20.770
Let me ask you something.

37
00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:27.350
Let's say we have a GDG and then we submit a job which creates a plus one generation.

38
00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:31.550
And then we run another job which creates another plus one generation.

39
00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:36.070
If we want to reference the data that we were originally referencing before those two jobs

40
00:02:36.070 --> 00:02:40.150
ran, what generation would we refer to?

41
00:02:40.150 --> 00:02:44.650
Because that data started out as the current version, zero, it got moved to the minus one

42
00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:46.870
version when we created the new one.

43
00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:50.350
And when we ran that second job, it got moved to the minus two slot.

44
00:02:50.350 --> 00:02:53.290
So the correct answer is minus two.

45
00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:56.290
There's an easier, more future-proof way of doing this though.

46
00:02:56.290 --> 00:03:01.130
We can also give the absolute data set name which specifies the generation number and

47
00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:02.930
version number.

48
00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:07.750
Now when we first start a GDG, both the generation and version numbers are zero.

49
00:03:07.750 --> 00:03:16.650
So you get the data set name dot G0000V00 which is why people sometimes refer to this as the

50
00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:18.050
GUVU number.

51
00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:22.090
As in, yeah, the relative number will change at the end of the week so when you document

52
00:03:22.090 --> 00:03:26.050
where you saw that problem, make sure you write down the GUVU number so we can find

53
00:03:26.050 --> 00:03:28.330
it later on.

54
00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:32.330
I said you can specify how many generations you want to keep around and here's where that

55
00:03:32.330 --> 00:03:35.130
option is along with a few others.

56
00:03:35.130 --> 00:03:40.090
You specify the name of the GDG data set, then we specify the limit.

57
00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:42.690
That's how many generations we want to keep around.

58
00:03:42.690 --> 00:03:46.730
You can also specify just how we want the generations to be handled.

59
00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:52.190
This no empty parameter means that when the limit of GDGs is hit, the oldest one falls

60
00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:55.330
off the end and everything else moves down a slot.

61
00:03:55.330 --> 00:03:58.670
And this repeats every time a new generation gets added.

62
00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:04.030
If you specify the empty parameter, once your limit gets hit, all the generations get deleted

63
00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:08.470
so you probably want to go with the no empty parameter in there.

64
00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:14.470
You can also specify if you want the generations to be first in, first out, or last in, first

65
00:04:14.470 --> 00:04:17.830
out with the FIFO or LIFO parameter.

66
00:04:17.830 --> 00:04:23.670
LIFO is the default, though you can specify this using the GDG order JCL keyword for a

67
00:04:23.670 --> 00:04:24.770
job.

68
00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:27.470
And that's a little introduction to GDGs.

69
00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:28.710
I told you it would be exciting.
